.TH WIILANDD 1 "August 2026" "wiiland" "WiiLand Manual"
.SH NAME
wiilandd \- bridge Wii Remote input to Wayland through Linux uinput
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wiilandd
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B wiilandd --device
.RI number | /sys/path
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wiilandd
is the Wayland-native input bridge for WiiLand. It reads Wii Remote events
through
.B libxwiimote
and creates virtual Linux uinput controllers. Wayland compositors consume these
virtual evdev devices through libinput, while native games, SDL, Wine, and
Proton can consume them through the normal Linux input stack.
.PP
This program does not use X11 and does not require XWayland. It is intended to
replace the old Xorg input-driver path for Wayland sessions.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR -l , " --list"
List connected Wii Remote devices and exit. Combine with
.B --verbose
to include devtype and extension attributes from sysfs. This does not create
uinput devices.
.TP
.BR --version
Print the wiilandd version and exit.
.TP
.BR -d , " --device " number | /sys/path
Bridge one device instead of monitoring all connected and hotplugged devices.
A number refers to the output of
.BR --list .
.TP
.BR -p , " --profile " gamepad | desktop | both
Select the virtual input profile. The default
.B gamepad
profile creates a virtual controller for games. The
.B desktop
profile creates a virtual mouse/keyboard device: D-pad repeat and valid IR
events move the pointer, A/B click, +/- send Enter/Escape, Home sends the
compositor overview key, and 1/2 send Page Down/Page Up.
.TP
.BR --backend " " uinput
Select the input backend. Only
.B uinput
is currently supported; it is the default compositor-independent Wayland path.
.TP
.BR --pointer-speed " " 1-127
Set the desktop profile pointer step in pixels per repeat tick. The default is
16.
.TP
.BR --ir-speed " " 1-127
Set the desktop profile IR pointer gain. The default is 8, matching one eighth
of the raw IR camera delta.
.TP
.BR --ir-deadzone " " 0-127
Suppress desktop profile IR pointer deltas smaller than this value after gain
scaling. The default is 0.
.TP
.BR --ir-smoothing " " 0-95
Smooth desktop profile IR pointer coordinates by retaining this percentage of
the previous filtered position. The default is 0.
.TP
.BR --ir-tracking " " dual | centroid | first
Select IR blob tracking. The default
.B dual
uses the midpoint of the widest valid IR-dot pair, avoiding source-order swaps
when both sensor-bar LEDs are visible.
.B centroid
averages every valid dot.
.B first
preserves the legacy first-valid-source tracker.
.TP
.BR --ir-aim-mapping " " relative | absolute
Select IR aim mapping. The default
.B relative
emits camera-like motion deltas and requires no screen calibration.
.B absolute
maps the selected IR point through
.B ir-screen-*
calibration bounds for users who want Wii-menu-style positional aim.
.TP
.BR -c , " --config " path
Load a key=value configuration file. Command-line options override loaded
configuration.
.TP
.BR --no-config
Skip the default configuration file.
.TP
.BR -n , " --dry-run"
Enumerate and open devices without creating /dev/uinput devices or emitting
virtual input. Useful for smoke tests and service diagnostics.
.TP
.BR --check-config
Load configuration, apply command-line overrides, and exit without enumerating
devices. Useful for packaging, CI, and service preflight checks.
.TP
.BR --dump-config
Print the fully resolved configuration after loading files and applying
command-line overrides, then exit.
.TP
.BR --axis-map
Print the virtual gamepad axis and button mapping used by the gamepad profile,
then exit. This is a hardware-validation aid and does not enumerate devices.
.TP
.BR --validation-checklist
Print the required real-hardware validation matrix, then exit. This is a
hardware-report aid and does not enumerate devices.
.TP
.BR --doctor
Print runtime readiness diagnostics for the current session,
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ,
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
the resolved Wayland socket path,
.BR /dev/uinput ,
backend, and profile, then exit. This loads configuration like a normal daemon
start; combine with
.BR --no-config
to inspect only built-in defaults. This does not enumerate Wii devices.
.TP
.BR --self-test
Run deterministic profile, config, button-map, IR, and pointer-motion tests, then exit.
This mode does not enumerate devices or open
.BR /dev/uinput .
.TP
.BR --trace-events [ =all | keys | axes | ir | motion-plus ]
Print sequenced, monotonic-timestamped decoded libxwiimote event records before
forwarding them. Optional filters reduce output to key events, absolute-axis
events, IR events, or MotionPlus events. IR events are reported as
.B ir
with source coordinates; MotionPlus events are reported as
.B motion-plus
with X/Y/Z axis payloads. Intended for hardware validation reports; combine with
.B --dry-run
to inspect devices without creating virtual input.
.TP
.BR --calibrate-aim
Capture accelerometer zero and MotionPlus gyro bias from a flat, still Wii
Remote and print persistent
.B wiilandd.conf
key=value lines. Place the Wii Remote face down with buttons against a stable
surface for the sample window.
.TP
.BR --aim-calibration-duration " " 1-30
Set the
.B --calibrate-aim
sample window in seconds. The default is 8.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Print device lifecycle details.
.SH PERMISSIONS
The process must be allowed to read the hid-wiimote input devices and write to
.BR /dev/uinput .
Distributions usually provide input-device access through logind/udev. Access
to
.B /dev/uinput
may require a udev rule, a system service, or an input/uinput group policy.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Unless
.B --no-config
is set,
.B wiilandd
loads
.B /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
first, then
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
or
.B $HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
when present. Later files override earlier values. Use
.B --config
to load one explicit file instead of the default hierarchy. Help and list modes
do not load configuration, so diagnostics remain available even with a broken
config file. Supported keys are:
.TP
.B backend=uinput
Input backend. Only uinput is currently supported.
.TP
.B profile=gamepad|desktop|both
Default virtual input profile.
.TP
.B pointer-speed=1-127
Desktop profile pointer step.
.TP
.B ir-speed=1-127
Desktop profile IR pointer gain.
.TP
.B ir-deadzone=0-127
Desktop profile IR jitter deadzone after gain scaling.
.TP
.B ir-smoothing=0-95
Desktop profile IR smoothing percentage.
.TP
.B ir-tracking=dual|centroid|first
Choose the IR tracker used by both desktop IR pointer motion and IR motion aim.
The default
.B dual
tracks the midpoint of the widest valid source pair, so source index swaps do
not cause jumps when the sensor bar is visible.
.TP
.B ir-aim-mapping=relative|absolute
Choose whether IR aim emits relative deltas or calibrated absolute screen
position. The default
.B relative
needs no display setup and remains best for camera aim.
.TP
.B ir-screen-left|right|top|bottom=0-32767
Optional screen bounds used only when
.B ir-aim-mapping=absolute .
Right must exceed left and bottom must exceed top; invalid rectangles are
rejected during configuration validation. These values let users dial in TV
size, aspect ratio, overscan, and sensor-bar placement without making absolute
pointing mandatory.
.TP
.B aim-mode=off|mouse|right-stick
Enable modern motion aiming. The default
.B off
keeps the login/session-safe gamepad unchanged.
.B mouse
converts the selected sensor to relative
.BR REL_X / REL_Y
pointer aim.
.B right-stick
converts it to
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY
camera aim for Steam, Proton/Wine, SDL, and native games that read a standard
right stick.
.TP
.B aim-source=auto|ir|motion-plus|accelerometer
Select the motion-aim sensor. The default
.B auto
prefers valid IR camera deltas, then MotionPlus gyro axes, then accelerometer
tilt. Calibration offsets are applied when configured. Without
.B aim-accel-zero-*
keys, accelerometer aim keeps the compatibility fallback of taking the first
accelerometer event as neutral.
.TP
.B aim-activation=always|b|z|c
Choose whether motion aim is always active or gated by holding B, Nunchuk Z, or
Nunchuk C. The default is
.BR b .
.TP
.B aim-sensitivity=1-127
Motion aim output gain. The default is 16.
.TP
.B aim-deadzone=0-32767
Motion aim deadzone before gain. The default is 4.
.TP
.B aim-smoothing=0-95
Motion aim smoothing percentage. The default is 25.
.TP
.B aim-invert-x=yes|no
Invert motion aim X output. The default is no.
.TP
.B aim-invert-y=yes|no
Invert motion aim Y output. The default is no.
.TP
.B aim-accel-zero-x|y|z=-32768-32767
Persist the flat-surface accelerometer neutral point generated by
.BR --calibrate-aim .
When set, accelerometer aim subtracts this point instead of using first-event
neutral fallback.
.TP
.B aim-motion-plus-bias-x|y|z=-32768-32767
Persist the still MotionPlus gyro bias generated by
.BR --calibrate-aim .
When set, MotionPlus aim subtracts this bias before sensitivity, deadzone, and
smoothing are applied.
.TP
.B aim-calibration-duration=1-30
Default calibration sample window used by
.BR --calibrate-aim .
The default is 8.
.TP
.B desktop.a|b|plus|minus|home|one|two=ACTION
Override desktop button output. Actions are
.BR left-click ,
.BR right-click ,
.BR enter ,
.BR escape ,
.BR overview ,
.BR page-up ,
.BR page-down ,
and
.BR disabled .
.TP
.B device.<syspath-substring>.profile=gamepad|desktop|both
Override the profile for devices whose sysfs path contains the substring.
.TP
.B device-type.<devtype-substring>.profile=gamepad|desktop|both
Override the profile for devices whose hid-wiimote devtype contains the
substring, such as
.BR gen10 ,
.BR gen20 ,
.BR balanceboard ,
or
.BR procontroller .
Rules are evaluated in file order; later matching rules win.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B res/wiilandd.conf
Documented sample configuration for packaging and local copies.
.TP
.B res/70-wiiland-uinput.rules
udev rules for active-seat access to hid-wiimote event nodes and
.BR /dev/uinput .
.TP
.B res/wiilandd.service
systemd user service bound to
.BR graphical-session.target .
It uses
.B --check-config
as an
.B ExecCondition
before starting, so invalid configuration does not enter a restart loop.
After installation, enable it with
.BR "systemctl --user enable wiilandd.service" ;
start it immediately with
.BR "systemctl --user start wiilandd.service" .
.SH GAMEPAD MAPPING
The gamepad profile exposes core and extension buttons as evdev button codes and
extension sensors on stable evdev absolute axes so SDL, Wine/Proton, and native
input testers can validate each source separately.
.TP
.B Wii Remote
Core buttons use D-pad, South/East, Start/Select, Mode, and 1/2 button events.
Accelerometer X/Y/Z use
.BR ABS_THROTTLE ,
.BR ABS_RUDDER ,
and
.BR ABS_WHEEL .
.TP
.B Nunchuk
Stick X/Y use
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
accelerometer X/Y/Z use
.BR ABS_HAT1X ,
.BR ABS_HAT1Y ,
and
.BR ABS_HAT2X .
.TP
.B MotionPlus
X/Y/Z use
.BR ABS_GAS ,
.BR ABS_BRAKE ,
and
.BR ABS_HAT0X .
.TP
.B Motion aiming
When
.B aim-mode=right-stick
is enabled, IR, MotionPlus, or accelerometer aim is emitted on
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY .
When
.B aim-mode=mouse
is enabled, the same aim signal is emitted on
.BR REL_X / REL_Y
through a virtual desktop pointer device. This deliberately exposes generic
Linux input instead of spoofing a DualSense, DualShock, or Steam Controller.
.SS Motion aim calibration
Run
.BR "wiilandd --device 1 --calibrate-aim"
and keep the Wii Remote face down, buttons against a flat stable surface, for
the printed sample window. The command rejects short or unstable samples and
prints only the stable accelerometer and MotionPlus offsets it captured. Paste
the generated
.BR aim-accel-zero-* ,
.BR aim-motion-plus-bias-* ,
and
.B aim-calibration-duration
lines into the same config file that sets
.BR aim-mode .
The Qt control center exposes the same fields and a capture button in the
Validation tab.
.SS IR tracking and screen calibration
The default IR path is calibration-free relative tracking. It uses the
.B dual
tracker to pick the midpoint of the widest valid IR-source pair, which is stable
when the sensor bar reports its two LEDs in different source slots between
frames. Users can switch to
.B centroid
for multi-source averaging or
.B first
for legacy behavior.
.PP
Screen/corner calibration is optional. Set
.B ir-aim-mapping=absolute
and provide
.BR ir-screen-left ,
.BR ir-screen-right ,
.BR ir-screen-top ,
and
.B ir-screen-bottom
when a game or setup benefits from Wii-menu-style positional aim. Relative
desktop pointer motion and relative IR aim continue to work without these
bounds, so diverse screens and top/bottom sensor-bar placements do not become a
setup requirement.
.PP
.PP
.TP
.B Classic Controller
Left stick uses
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
right stick uses
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY ;
analog triggers use
.BR ABS_Z / ABS_RZ .
.TP
.B Wii U Pro Controller
Left stick uses
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
right stick uses
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY .
ZL/ZR are reported as
.BR BTN_TL2 / BTN_TR2
button events.
.TP
.B Guitar
Stick X/Y use
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
whammy and fret board use the valid generic uinput axes
.BR ABS_HAT3X
and
.BR ABS_HAT3Y ;
strum/fret buttons use dedicated guitar button events, and +/Home use
.BR BTN_START / BTN_MODE .
.TP
.B Drums
Pad X/Y use
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
left/right cymbals use
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY ;
left/right/far-right toms use
.BR ABS_Z ,
.BR ABS_RZ ,
and
.BR ABS_HAT3X ;
bass and hi-hat pedals use
.BR ABS_HAT3Y
and
.BR ABS_MISC ;
+/- use
.BR BTN_START / BTN_SELECT .
.TP
.B Balance Board
The four weight sensors use
.BR ABS_PRESSURE ,
.BR ABS_DISTANCE ,
.BR ABS_TILT_X ,
and
.BR ABS_TILT_Y .
.SH BACKEND
The default backend is Linux uinput because it is compositor-independent and is
already consumed by Wayland compositors and game stacks. WiiLand opens only the
hid-wiimote interfaces needed by the selected profile and aim source. Optional
extension-open failures do not discard an otherwise usable remote, and bounded
event draining keeps desktop pointer repeat responsive during continuous sensor
traffic. The gamepad profile is safe as a login default; use
.B --profile=desktop
or
.B --profile=both
when the Wii Remote should also drive the desktop pointer and keyboard. A future
optional libei backend can provide compositor-mediated emulated input where
available.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wiiland (7),
.BR libxwiimote (7),
.BR xwiishow (1)
